    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The glycemic index (GI) measures a food's effect on blood sugar levels. High GI foods (75+ on the scale of 0-100) are rapidly digested, causing a spike in blood sugar and releasing insulin to regulate it. This can lead to insulin resistance and negative health effects. On the other hand, low GI foods (55 or less) are digested more slowly, keeping blood sugar levels stable and reducing insulin demand. By choosing low GI foods, you can help regulate your blood sugar and insulin levels, promoting overall health and reducing the risk of chronic diseases.",
